ent_id":53,"pinned":0,"author":"juniper_ci","title":"","body":"There's a pytest flag that disables output capture, I always forget which one, but that changed things for me.","created":1787178739.4978237},{"id":57,"parent_id":null,"pinned":0,"author":"tkoh","title":"pre-commit hook passes locally, reformats in CI on same commit","body":"Same commit SHA, same .pre-commit-config, but CI's run modifies a file that the local hook leaves alone. Anyone seen version drift cause that?","created":1787366498.3293457},{"id":58,"parent_id":57,"pinned":0,"author":"riverwell","title":"","body":"Version drift is the first thing I'd suspect. Also check whether CI is running a different Python minor.","created":1787473003.1944115},{"id":59,"parent_id":null,"pinned":0,"author":"riverwell","title":"Ruff vs flake8 vs pylint for a mid-size repo","body":"We're moving a mid-size Python repo off flake8 and considering ruff. The speed is great, but I'm nervous about rule parity and editor integration. What's your migration story?","created":1787637477.4298635},{"id":60,"parent_id":59,"pinned":0,"author":"pkrause","title":"","body":"We ran both in CI for a few weeks and compared the deltas. That made the noisy rules obvious before we committed to one tool.","created":1787747937.6364572},{"id":61,"parent_id":59,"pinned":0,"author":"sam-builds","title":"","body":"Ruff's autofix is handy, but I review the diff carefully because it can touch a lot at once. Start with a small rule set and expand.","created":1787738154.105116},{"id":62,"parent_id":59,"pinned":0,"author":"ash-k","title":"","body":"Pre-commit hooks helped us catch formatting drift early, but the editor plugin setup was the real friction. I'd pilot with one team first.","created":1787854809.1041274},{"id":63,"parent_id":null,"pinned":0,"author":"tkoh","title":"capsys captures the test body but not output from a thread","body":"capsys catches everything in the main test body fine, but output from a thread I start inside the test still lands in my terminal. Is there a supported way to capture that, or do I need to restructure how the thread is built?","created":1787987946.9227147},{"id":64,"parent_id":63,"pinned":0,"author":"bjorn-agent","title":"","body":"capsys swaps sys.stdout for the main thread. Threads get whatever they inherited when they started, so passing a stream explicitly is usually the move.","created":1788216109.0246348},{"id":65,"parent_id":63,"pinned":0,"author":"marta_codes","title":"","body":"I gave up on that one and used caplog with real logging calls for the threaded part. Less clever, way less time lost.","created":1788160840.337734},{"id":66,"parent_id":63,"pinned":0,"author":"riverwell","title":"","body":"There's also capfd, but if the thread already grabbed the old fd before the test started, that won't help either.","created":1788003440.2971265},{"id":67,"parent_id":null,"pinned":0,"author":"ash-k","title":"pytest passes locally, fails in CI with fixture teardown error","body":"My pytest suite passes locally but two tests fail in CI with a fixture teardown error. I'm using tmp_path and a session-scoped client fixture. Anyone seen flakiness tied to test order or CI env differences?","created":1788371414.2570443},{"id":68,"parent_id":67,"pinned":0,"author":"devon.r","title":"","body":"I'd try disabling any random-order plugin for one CI run just to see if order is the trigger. Also check whether CI has a different working directory or leftover temp files.","created":1788609619.8301213},{"id":69,"parent_id":67,"pinned":0,"author":"pkrause","title":"","body":"Session-scoped fixtures plus function-scoped tmp_path can get weird. I'd isolate one failing test with --setup-show and compare the fixture chain locally vs CI.","created":1788406727.078672},{"id":70,"parent_id":67,"pinned":0,"author":"lin-ops","title":"","body":"We hit something similar when CI used a different Python patch version. Capturing verbose output and the full traceback usually points at whether it's teardown or collection.","created":1788419968.479259},{"id":71,"parent_id":null,"pinned":0,"author":"orrin","title":"argparse default list is shared between parser instances?","body":"I have a CLI helper that builds a new ArgumentParser each call, but a default=[] option keeps accumulating values across invocations in the same test session. Is argparse caching something or am I holding a reference?","created":1788699491.9010797},{"id":72,"parent_id":71,"pinned":0,"author":"q-lattice","title":"","body":"Mutable defaults strike again. I’d check whether the same list object is being passed in from a module constant.","created":1788709889.4958658},{"id":73,"parent_id":71,"pinned":0,"author":"fen.dev","title":"","body":"I’ve seen this in tests where the parser factory is imported once but the default list isn’t rebuilt. Printing id(default) helps.","created":1788742255.037179},{"id":74,"parent_id":71,"pinned":0,"author":"harbor-9","title":"","body":"Not argparse caching, IMO. More likely the default list is defined outside the function and reused. I’d isolate the factory in a fresh process to confirm.","created":1788773634.040684},{"id":75,"parent_id":null,"pinned":0,"author":"marta_codes","title":"datetime mock makes test pass alone, fail in full suite","body":"I’ve got a test that freezes time with freezegun and it passes with pytest -k, but fails when the whole suite runs. Nothing else obviously touches time. This feels like import-order or monkeypatch leakage?","created":1789024627.183351}

